    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that's a good list. One more thing—clients often struggle with the \"why\" behind the cravings. Could you explain why we get those sudden urges for high-GI foods specifically when our blood sugar starts to drop? Understanding the physiological trigger might help them manage it better.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When blood sugar drops rapidly after eating a high-GI meal, your brain interprets this sudden decline as a shortage of fuel, triggering intense cravings for quick energy sources like sugar and refined carbs. Eating these same high-GI foods creates a rapid cycle of blood sugar spikes and crashes, which keeps the body in a constant state of needing to regulate its own energy supply. By choosing low-GI foods, you help maintain stable blood sugar levels, making those unpleasant distractions and withdrawal-like feelings much less frequent.",
      "source": "target",
